========================================================================================
 policy trending: Policy operation trending
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: policy count is in the tens of thousands and grows with
 every application/environment pair. Is that growth accelerating, which policies are
 actually referenced, and is anything a leading indicator of policy lookup latency?

 STATUS: verified 2026-09-02. All 7 searches executed against real Vault audit data
 containing policy-management events, and all returned rows. Note that an audit stream
 with no policy writes in the window will render these empty, correctly.

 NOTE ON EMPTY RESULTS: the searches that read policy MANAGEMENT paths need somebody to
 have written a policy inside the search window. Many estates go weeks without one, so
 empty is common and correct. The searches that read 'auth.policies{}' instead describe
 policies in force on every login, so those have data whenever there is any traffic. If
 you want a quick signal that this file works at all, start with those.

 Assumes the sample loaded as index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit with KV_MODE=json.

 Nested JSON fields are referenced as 'request.path' (single quotes) inside eval/where,
 and as request.path=... in the base search.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHICH DATA SOURCE ANSWERS WHICH QUESTION. READ THIS BEFORE BUILDING PANELS.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 There are two different things people mean by "policy metrics" and they live in two
 different systems.

 AUDIT LOG (this file) answers WHAT HAPPENED:
   - who created, updated, read, listed or deleted a policy, and when
   - which policies were attached to the token that made a request
   - how the distinct policy count is growing over time
   These are events. Every one of them is a fact about a specific request.

 TELEMETRY (Prometheus / StatsD, NOT this file) answers HOW EXPENSIVE IT WAS:
   - `vault.policy.get_policy`      policy lookup latency
   - `vault.policy.set_policy`      policy write latency
   - `vault.core.fetch_acl_and_token`, `vault.expire.*`, `vault.runtime.*`

 `vault.policy.get_policy` IS NOT IN THE AUDIT LOG. It is a runtime timing metric emitted
 by Vault's telemetry subsystem, on the interval configured in the `telemetry` stanza. It
 has no request id, no path, no principal, and no corresponding audit entry. There is no
 way to derive it, estimate it, or stand in for it using audit events, and a panel that
 pretends otherwise is worse than a missing panel because it looks answered.

 So: THE LEADING INDICATOR FOR POLICY LOOKUP LATENCY IS A TELEMETRY PANEL, NOT AN AUDIT
 PANEL. If you want it, enable the telemetry stanza with a Prometheus endpoint and build
 it in Grafana against `vault_policy_get_policy` (the Prometheus rendering of that metric,
 exposed as a summary with quantiles). Point the audit panels below at the same dashboard
 next to it. What audit CAN contribute is the correlate: policy count and policy churn
 over the same time axis, so you can see whether a latency rise tracks a policy-count
 rise. That correlation is genuinely useful. It is not a substitute for the metric.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.0  Sanity check: is policy activity being audited at all, and on what paths?
       RUN THIS FIRST. If it returns nothing, every search below returns nothing, and
       the reason is upstream: either no policy changes happened in the window, or the
       audit device is filtered. Do not read an empty result as "no policy churn".
       ACL policies live at sys/policies/acl/<name>. The pre-1.0 path sys/policy/<name>
       still works and still shows up in audit logs, so match both.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    (request.path="sys/policies/*" OR request.path="sys/policy*")
| stats count AS events,
        dc(request.path) AS distinct_paths,
        min(_time) AS earliest, max(_time) AS latest
        BY request.operation
| eval earliest=strftime(earliest,"%F %T"), latest=strftime(latest,"%F %T")


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.1  Policy operation rate over time, split by operation
       The trend line. `list` dominating is normal (the UI and most tooling list before
       they act). A rise in `create`/`update` is real churn.

       Vault writes both create and update as operation=update on these paths, so treat
       "write" as one class rather than trying to split new from changed. Splitting them
       needs the create/delete events in 6.5, not the operation verb.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    (request.path="sys/policies/acl/*" OR request.path="sys/policy/*")
| eval policy_op = case(
      'request.operation'=="read",   "read",
      'request.operation'=="list",   "list",
      'request.operation'=="update", "write",
      'request.operation'=="delete", "delete",
      true(),                        'request.operation')
| timechart span=1d count BY policy_op


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.2  Policy operations by namespace
       Policies are per-namespace. This is the churn-ownership table: which team is
       actually generating the growth. Note the root-namespace coalesce; without it
       root-namespace policy activity silently vanishes into a null group.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    (request.path="sys/policies/acl/*" OR request.path="sys/policy/*")
| eval namespace = if('request.namespace.path'=="" OR isnull('request.namespace.path'),
                      "root", 'request.namespace.path')
| eval qualified_path = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats count AS ops,
        dc(qualified_path) AS distinct_policies_touched,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="update")) AS writes,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="delete")) AS deletes,
        max(_time) AS last_op
        BY namespace
| eval last_op = strftime(last_op, "%F %T")
| sort - ops


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.3  Top policies by reference count
       A different and better measure of "is this policy used" than policy read
       operations. 'auth.policies{}' is the policy set attached to the token on EVERY
       request, so this counts how often each policy was actually in force, not how
       often somebody looked at its definition.

       `default` will be at the top of every estate and is not interesting. The
       interesting end of this table is the BOTTOM: a policy that exists and is never
       attached to anything is the policy equivalent of an unread secret. But see the
       warning under 6.4 before you act on the bottom of this list.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
| eval namespace = if('request.namespace.path'=="" OR isnull('request.namespace.path'),
                      "root", 'request.namespace.path')
| stats count AS references,
        dc(auth.display_name) AS distinct_principals,
        max(_time) AS last_referenced
        BY auth.policies{} namespace
| rename auth.policies{} AS policy
| where policy != "default"
| eval last_referenced = strftime(last_referenced, "%F %T")
| sort - references
| head 100


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.4  Growth in distinct policy count over time
       The number the capacity conversation needs: is the policy count growing linearly
       or accelerating? Bucket by day, count distinct policies seen in force that day,
       then take a running maximum as the floor on estate size.

       THIS IS A FLOOR, NOT A CENSUS, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. It counts policies
       SEEN IN USE in the window. A policy that exists but was not attached to any
       request that day does not appear. Rarely-used and entirely-unused policies are
       invisible here for exactly the reason unread secrets are invisible in the stale-secret searches: there
       is no event to find. The true count comes from `vault policy list` per namespace,
       the same shape of one-time walk the secret-count searches uses for secrets, and the honest version of
       this panel plots both lines: inventoried policies, and policies seen in use.
       The gap between them is the dead-policy population.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
| rename auth.policies{} AS policy
| where policy != "default"
| bin _time span=1d
| stats dc(policy) AS policies_seen_that_day BY _time
| streamstats max(policies_seen_that_day) AS policy_count_floor
| eval day = strftime(_time, "%F")
| table day policies_seen_that_day policy_count_floor


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.5  Policy creation and deletion churn, net growth per week
       Where the growth is actually coming from. Requires policy management events in
       the window; see the caveat at the top of this file.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    (request.path="sys/policies/acl/*" OR request.path="sys/policy/*")
    (request.operation=update OR request.operation=delete)
| eval policy = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| bin _time span=7d
| stats count(eval('request.operation'=="update")) AS writes,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="delete")) AS deletes,
        dc(policy) AS distinct_policies
        BY _time
| eval net_change = writes - deletes
| eval week = strftime(_time, "%F")
| table week writes deletes net_change distinct_policies


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.6  Who is writing policies
       Policy churn at this scale is almost always machine-generated. This says which
       pipeline or principal is generating it, which is the conversation that actually
       reduces the count.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    (request.path="sys/policies/acl/*" OR request.path="sys/policy/*")
    request.operation=update
| eval namespace = if('request.namespace.path'=="" OR isnull('request.namespace.path'),
                      "root", 'request.namespace.path')
| eval policy = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats count AS writes,
        dc(policy) AS distinct_policies,
        values(request.remote_address) AS source_ips,
        max(_time) AS last_write
        BY auth.display_name namespace
| eval last_write = strftime(last_write, "%F %T")
| sort - writes


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6.7  NOT ANSWERABLE FROM AUDIT LOGS. Listed so nobody builds a fake version.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Policy lookup LATENCY              -> telemetry `vault.policy.get_policy`
 Policy write LATENCY               -> telemetry `vault.policy.set_policy`
 ACL evaluation cost per request    -> telemetry `vault.core.fetch_acl_and_token`
 Memory held by the policy store    -> telemetry `vault.runtime.alloc_bytes`, plus
                                       cluster memory metrics
 Total policy count INCLUDING
   policies nobody uses             -> `vault policy list` per namespace, a one-time
                                       walk in the shape of the secret-count searches's inventory. Not audit.
 Policy CONTENT, and therefore
   "which policies are near-identical
   and could be templated"          -> the policy documents themselves, read from the
                                       API. Audit records that a policy was written,
                                       never what was in it.

 That last one is worth saying plainly, because it is the question underneath the
 fifteen-thousand-policy problem. If policies are being generated one per
 application/environment pair, the fix is templating, and deciding whether templating
 is viable requires diffing the policy bodies. No audit search can do that. Pull the
 policy documents, hash the normalised bodies, and cluster them. Audit trending tells
 you the problem is growing. It cannot tell you what to collapse.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
